description:
memory.rs:191-207:

    fn recompute_address_books_with_contents(&mut self, account_id: &str) {
        let cards = self
            .objects
            .get(&("ContactCard", account_id.to_owned()))
            .cloned()
            .unwrap_or_default();

        let mut set: HashSet<Id> = HashSet::new();
        for value in cards.values() {
            if let Some(map) = value.get("addressBookIds").and_then(|v| v.as_object()) {
                for k in map.keys() {
                    set.insert(Id::from(k.as_str()));
                }
            }
        }
        self.aux_mut(account_id).address_books_with_contents = set;
    }

The `.cloned()` deep-clones the entire HashMap<Id, serde_json::Value> for the account — every Value is a tree of Maps and Vecs, every clone is O(total card payload). This runs on every ContactCard create/update/destroy (see lines 639-641, 697-699, 727-729, 789).

The clone is there because the function takes &mut self and the borrow checker would otherwise complain about reading self.objects while later writing self.aux. Idiomatic fixes:

1. Two-phase: compute the new HashSet into a local in one borrow, then move it into self.aux in a second borrow. The HashSet contains owned Ids, so the second borrow is free of the first:

    let new_set = self.objects.get(&("ContactCard", account_id.to_owned()))
        .map(|cards| {
            let mut set = HashSet::new();
            for value in cards.values() { ... }
            set
        })
        .unwrap_or_default();
    self.aux.entry(account_id.to_owned()).or_default().address_books_with_contents = new_set;

2. NLL split borrow on the same struct: extract the read into a local that borrows self.objects, drop the borrow before touching self.aux.

The current shape is the textbook ".clone() to escape the borrow checker rather than rethinking the design". The fix is mechanical and removes one O(card-payload) clone from every contacts mutation.

Same pattern likely exists in canonical jmap-mail-server (analogous derived index recompute) — verify and propagate.
